\title{The Retention Knee of Attention Profiles:\\
Additive Delay, Multiplicative Decay Tax,\\
and the Gauge Freedom of the Master Knee}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-09-01}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous theory of the \emph{retention knee} of a discrete attention profile: for a nonnegative weight sequence $w=(w_i)_{i\ge 0}$ and a gate $\tau$, the knee $\ks(w,\tau)$ is the least number of leading positions whose cumulative mass reaches $\tau$. The motivation is an empirical five-domain table measured at a fixed context length and gate --- code $12$, English prose $20$, mathematics $20$, German prose $24$, French prose $40$ --- and the resulting dispute over whether the cross-domain ``tokenizer tax'' is an additive step ($+4$) or a multiplicative factor ($\times 2$).

We prove that the two candidate mechanisms are structurally distinct. A \emph{delay} by $d$ inert positions shifts the knee by exactly $+d$ at every positive gate. An $m$-th \emph{root of the decay ratio} multiplies the knee by $m$ up to a ceiling: if the fine profile has geometric knee $B$, the coarse one has knee $\lceil B/m\rceil$, with equality $B=mA$ precisely when $m\mid B$. For a genuine root tax with $m\ge 2$ the gap between the two knees is unbounded as the gate tightens, so no fixed additive constant can describe it.

We sharpen the multiplicative law to the two-sided integer bound $B\le mA<B+m$ and deduce that an English knee of $20$ under a square-root tax forces a French knee in $\{39,40\}$: the reported value $40$ is correct up to one position and is singled out only by a parity assumption that a coarse grid cannot verify.

Finally we study whether one \emph{master} profile generates the whole table under integer tax exponents. It does, non-uniquely. The master knee $120$ works with exponents $(10,6,6,5,3)$ and is minimal among masters dividing every entry exactly; but exact divisibility is not required by the ceiling law, and the master knee $118$ reproduces the same table with the same exponents. An exhaustive finite search shows no master below $118$ suffices, and a complete interval analysis shows the admissible masters for the exponent vector $(10,6,5,3)$ are exactly $\{118,119,120\}$; hence $118$ and $120$ are observationally indistinguishable on this data. We also prove a rigidity theorem --- exact multiplicativity at \emph{all} gates forces the taxed retention curve to be a block dilation of the untaxed one, with no geometric hypothesis --- and a tail-class separation theorem showing heavy-tailed profiles outgrow every geometric profile by an unbounded factor.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} retention knee, attention profile, geometric tail, ceiling division, tokenizer tax, multiplicative rigidity, tail-class dichotomy.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Consider any system that must decide, position by position, how much of a long context to consult. Its attention weights over the context, sorted in decreasing order, form a probability profile. The practical question --- \emph{how many positions must be kept to preserve a prescribed fraction of the attention mass?} --- has a single-number answer, the \emph{knee}, and that number depends systematically on the domain being read.

At a fixed context length of $1024$ positions and a fixed gate, the following table has been measured:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
domain & code & English prose & mathematics & German prose & French prose\\
\midrule
$\ks$ & $12$ & $20$ & $20$ & $24$ & $40$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two features demand explanation. German exceeds English by exactly $+4$; French exceeds English by exactly $\times 2$. These are different kinds of statement, and if both are instances of a single ``tokenizer tax'', that tax is not a single mechanism.

The French entry is the hard-won one. On an extended grid at gate $0.98$ the measured retained masses were

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
$k$ & $36$ & $40$ & $48$ & $56$ & $64$\\
\midrule
retained & $0.9795$ & $0.9830$ & $0.9855$ & $0.9896$ & $0.9916$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

so $k=36$ fails the gate and $k=40$ passes it. Two pre-registered hypotheses were in play: that a knee exists within the extended grid (confirmed), and that it lies near $28$--$32$ (refuted).

\subsection{Contributions}

Section~\ref{sec:defs} sets up profiles, retention and the knee. Section~\ref{sec:delay} proves the additive law. Section~\ref{sec:mult} solves the geometric knee in closed form, proves the root law, its exactness criterion, and the sharp two-sided bound. Section~\ref{sec:french} applies these to the French entry. Section~\ref{sec:master} analyses the master-knee reconstruction and establishes its exact gauge freedom. Section~\ref{sec:rigid} proves multiplicative rigidity. Section~\ref{sec:tails} proves the tail-class separation. Section~\ref{sec:grids} treats measurement grids. Sections~\ref{sec:disc}--\ref{sec:future} discuss consequences and open directions. All statements are exact; no asymptotic or statistical approximation is used.

\section{Profiles, retention and the knee}\label{sec:defs}

\begin{definition}[Profile]
A \emph{profile} is a function $w:\N\to\R$. It is \emph{nonnegative} if $w_i\ge 0$ for all $i$, and \emph{strictly positive} if $w_i>0$ for all $i$. Monotonicity and normalisation are not required except where stated; in applications $w$ is the decreasing rearrangement of an attention row and sums to $1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retained mass]
The \emph{retained mass} of the first $k$ positions is
\[
\Rw(k)=\sum_{i<k} w_i,\qquad \Rw(0)=0,\qquad \Rw(k+1)=\Rw(k)+w_k.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee]
For a gate $\tau\in\R$,
\[
\ks(w,\tau)=\inf\{\,k\in\N:\ \tau\le \Rw(k)\,\},
\]
with $\inf\emptyset=0$. Every result either assumes the gate is reachable or derives reachability.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Delay]
For $d\in\N$, $(\delayp_d w)_i = 0$ if $i<d$ and $w_{i-d}$ if $i\ge d$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Geometric and heavy profiles]
For $r\in[0,1)$, $\geomp(r)_i=(1-r)r^i$. The \emph{heavy} (telescoping) profile is $\heavyp_i=\dfrac{1}{(i+1)(i+2)}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity]\label{prop:mono}
If $w$ is nonnegative then $\Rw$ is monotone nondecreasing.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
For $a\le b$, $\Rw(b)-\Rw(a)$ is a sum of nonnegative terms.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Knee specification]\label{prop:spec}
If the gate set is nonempty then $\tau\le \Rw(\ks(w,\tau))$ and $\tau>\Rw(j)$ for every $j<\ks(w,\tau)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Membership of the infimum of a nonempty subset of $\N$ in that subset, plus minimality.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Characterisation]\label{prop:char}
If $\tau\le \Rw(n)$ and $\tau>\Rw(j)$ for all $j<n$, then $\ks(w,\tau)=n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The first hypothesis gives $\ks\le n$; if $\ks<n$ then $\tau\le \Rw(\ks)$ contradicts the second at $j=\ks$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Persistence]\label{prop:persist}
For nonnegative $w$ with reachable gate, $\tau\le \Rw(k)$ for every $k\ge \ks(w,\tau)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Combine Propositions~\ref{prop:mono} and~\ref{prop:spec}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Domination]\label{prop:dom}
If $\Rw(k)\le R_v(k)$ for all $k$ and $w$'s gate is reachable, then $\ks(v,\tau)\le \ks(w,\tau)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\tau\le \Rw(\ks(w,\tau))\le R_v(\ks(w,\tau))$, so $\ks(w,\tau)$ lies in $v$'s gate set.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Self-calibration]\label{prop:selfcal}
If $w$ is strictly positive then $\Rw$ is strictly increasing and $\ks(w,\Rw(k))=k$ for every $k$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\Rw(k+1)-\Rw(k)=w_k>0$; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:char} with $n=k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Density lower bound]
If $w_i\le p$ for all $i$ with $p>0$ and the gate is reachable, then $\tau/p\le \ks(w,\tau)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\tau\le \Rw(\ks)\le \ks p$.
\end{proof}

\section{The additive law: delays}\label{sec:delay}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:delay}
$R_{\delayp_d w}(d+k)=\Rw(k)$ for all $k$, and $R_{\delayp_d w}(k)=0$ for $k\le d$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The second statement is immediate. The first is induction on $k$: the base case is the second statement at $k=d$, and the step adds $(\delayp_d w)_{d+n}=w_n$ to both sides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additive tokenizer tax]\label{thm:delay}
Let $\tau>0$ and suppose $w$'s gate set is nonempty. Then
\[
\ks(\delayp_d w,\tau)=d+\ks(w,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:delay}, $R_{\delayp_d w}(d+\ks(w,\tau))=\Rw(\ks(w,\tau))\ge\tau$. For $j<d+\ks(w,\tau)$: if $j\le d$ then $R_{\delayp_d w}(j)=0<\tau$; otherwise $j=d+t$ with $t<\ks(w,\tau)$ and $R_{\delayp_d w}(j)=\Rw(t)<\tau$. Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:char}.
\end{proof}

The shift is exact and gate-independent: the same $d$ for every $\tau>0$. This is the precise content of a ``$+4$'' law.

\section{The multiplicative law: geometric tails}\label{sec:mult}

\subsection{The geometric knee}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:geomret}
$R_{\geomp(r)}(k)=1-r^k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction: $R(k+1)=(1-r^k)+(1-r)r^k=1-r^{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Geometric knee]
For tail budget $t=1-\tau$, $\kg(r,t)=\inf\{k:\ r^k\le t\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:adj}
For $r<1$ and $t>0$ the set $\{k:r^k\le t\}$ is nonempty, and for $0\le r<1$, $t>0$,
\[
\kg(r,t)\le n \iff r^n\le t.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Nonemptiness is $r^n\to 0$. If $\kg\le n$ then $r^n\le r^{\kg}\le t$ by antitonicity; conversely $r^n\le t$ puts $n$ in the set.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Gate translation]\label{prop:trans}
$\ks(\geomp(r),\tau)=\kg(r,1-\tau)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:geomret}, $\tau\le R(k)\iff r^k\le 1-\tau$; the gate sets coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Calibration]\label{prop:cal}
For $0<r<1$, $\kg(r,r^n)=n$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\le$ is Lemma~\ref{lem:adj}. If $\kg<n$ then $r^{n-1}\le r^n$, contradicting $r<1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For $0<r<1$ and $t>0$, $\kg(r,t)=\left\lceil \log t/\log r\right\rceil_{+}$, the nonnegative ceiling.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $\log r<0$, $r^k\le t\iff k\log r\le\log t\iff \log t/\log r\le k$. The gate set is the set of naturals above $\log t/\log r$, whose least element is the ceiling.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Ideal knee]
$\kappa(r,t)=\dfrac{\log t}{\log r}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[One-position accuracy]\label{prop:acc}
For $0<r<1$ and $0<t\le 1$, $\ \kappa(r,t)\le \kg(r,t)<\kappa(r,t)+1$ and $\kappa(r,t)\ge 0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:closed} and $x\le\lceil x\rceil<x+1$; nonnegativity from $\log t\le 0$, $\log r<0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The root law}

\begin{theorem}[Root-of-ratio law]\label{thm:root}
Let $0\le r<1$, $t>0$, $m\ge 1$. Then $\kg(r^m,t)=\left\lceil \kg(r,t)/m\right\rceil$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every $n$, by Lemma~\ref{lem:adj} twice and $(r^m)^n=r^{mn}$,
\[
\kg(r^m,t)\le n\iff r^{mn}\le t\iff \kg(r,t)\le mn\iff \left\lceil \kg(r,t)/m\right\rceil\le n,
\]
the last step being the defining adjunction of ceiling division. Two naturals with the same down-set are equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness criterion]\label{thm:exact}
With $B=\kg(r,t)$ and $A=\kg(r^m,t)$: $\ B=mA \iff m\mid B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) trivial. ($\Leftarrow$) if $B=mc$ then $\lceil B/m\rceil=c$ and $mA=mc=B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Ceiling interval form]\label{prop:interval}
For $m\ge 1$ and $v\ge 1$: $\ \left\lceil B/m\right\rceil=v\iff m(v-1)<B\le mv$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$\lceil B/m\rceil\le v\iff B\le mv$ and $\lceil B/m\rceil\le v-1\iff B\le m(v-1)$; subtract.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp two-sided bound]\label{thm:twosided}
Let $0\le r<1$, $t>0$, $m\ge 1$, $B=\kg(r,t)$, $A=\kg(r^m,t)$. Then
\[
B\ \le\ mA\ <\ B+m.
\]
The multiplicative law holds with an error strictly smaller than the multiplier.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:root}, $A=\lceil B/m\rceil$. If $A=0$ then $B\le 0$ and both inequalities hold. Otherwise Proposition~\ref{prop:interval} with $v=A$ gives $mA-m<B\le mA$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\kappa(r^m,t)=\kappa(r,t)/m$ exactly, with no ceiling.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Gate invariance of the tax ratio]\label{thm:gateinv}
For $0<r_1,r_2<1$ and $0<t<1$,
\[
\frac{\kappa(r_1,t)}{\kappa(r_2,t)}=\frac{\log r_2}{\log r_1},
\]
independently of the gate $t$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\dfrac{\log t/\log r_1}{\log t/\log r_2}=\dfrac{\log r_2}{\log r_1}$, valid since $\log t\ne0$ and $\log r_i\ne0$.
\end{proof}

This is the most directly falsifiable prediction of the theory: the ratio of two domains' knees is a pure tail invariant, so it must be reproduced at every gate, the only gate dependence being the sub-unit rounding of Proposition~\ref{prop:acc}.

\subsection{No additive surrogate}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded root gap]\label{thm:gap}
Let $0<r<1$, $m\ge2$, $N\in\N$. Then there is $t>0$ with $N+\kg(r^m,t)\le \kg(r,t)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $t=r^{mN+m}$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:cal}, $\kg(r,t)=mN+m$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:root} and $m\mid mN+m$, $\kg(r^m,t)=N+1$. Since $m\ge2$, $N+(N+1)\le mN+m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A delay is not a root]\label{cor:notroot}
For $0<r<1$, $m\ge2$ and any fixed $d$, there is $t>0$ with $\kg(r,t)\ne d+\kg(r^m,t)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:gap} with $N=d+1$.
\end{proof}

Thus a ``$+4$'' law and a ``$\times2$'' law cannot both be manifestations of one mechanism.

\section{The French entry}\label{sec:french}

\begin{theorem}[Structural form of the verdict]\label{thm:fr40}
Suppose the French profile is geometric with ratio $r$ and the English profile is geometric with ratio $r^2$. If the English knee is $20$ and the French knee is even, then the French knee is exactly $40$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:exact} with $m=2$: evenness of $B$ gives $B=2A=40$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The French knee is $39$ or $40$]\label{thm:fr3940}
Under the same hypotheses but without the parity assumption, the French knee lies in $\{39,40\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided} with $m=2$, $A=20$: $B\le 40<B+2$, so $38<B\le 40$.
\end{proof}

The parity hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:fr40} is exactly the bit that removes $39$; it is not observable on the reported grid $\{36,40,48,56,64\}$, which never tests $39$.

\begin{theorem}[Bracketing from the reported data]\label{thm:bracketfr}
Let $w$ be nonnegative with $\Rw(36)=0.9795$ and $\Rw(40)=0.9830$. Then at gate $0.98$, $\ 36<\ks(w,0.98)\le 40$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$0.9795<0.98\le0.9830$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:grid}(4).
\end{proof}

\section{The master knee and its gauge freedom}\label{sec:master}

Suppose all five domains share one master geometric profile with ratio $r$ and knee $B=\kg(r,t)$, and each domain $j$ observes ratio $r^{m_j}$ for an integer \emph{tax exponent} $m_j\ge1$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:root} its knee is $\lceil B/m_j\rceil$.

\begin{theorem}[Reconstruction at $B=120$]\label{thm:120}
If the master knee is $120$, the exponent vector $(10,6,6,5,3)$ reproduces the table:
\[
\lceil 120/10\rceil=12,\quad \lceil120/6\rceil=20,\quad \lceil120/5\rceil=24,\quad \lceil120/3\rceil=40.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:root}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minimality among exact divisors]
If $B>0$ is a common multiple of $20$ and $24$ then $120\le B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{lcm}(20,24)=120$ divides $B>0$.
\end{proof}

This is what makes $120$ look canonical --- but it assumes exact divisibility, which the ceiling law does not impose.

\begin{theorem}[Reconstruction at $B=118$]\label{thm:118}
If the master knee is $118$, the \emph{same} exponent vector reproduces the \emph{same} table:
\[
\lceil118/10\rceil=12,\quad \lceil118/6\rceil=20,\quad \lceil118/5\rceil=24,\quad \lceil118/3\rceil=40.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$118/10=11.8$, $118/6=19.\overline{6}$, $118/5=23.6$, $118/3=39.\overline{3}$; take ceilings and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:root}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exponent bound]\label{lem:expbound}
If $m\ge1$, $\lceil B/m\rceil=v$ and $v\ge2$, then $m\le B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $m>B$ then $B\le m\cdot1$, so $\lceil B/m\rceil\le1<v$.
\end{proof}

Say $B$ \emph{realizes} $v$ if $\lceil B/m\rceil=v$ for some $m\ge1$, and $B$ \emph{covers the table} if it realizes each of $12,20,24,40$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:expbound} it suffices to search $1\le m\le B$, so covering is decidable by finite computation.

\begin{theorem}[Minimality of $118$]\label{thm:min118}
No $B<118$ covers the table: there are no $m_1,m_2,m_3,m_4\ge1$ with
\[
\lceil B/m_1\rceil=12,\quad \lceil B/m_2\rceil=20,\quad \lceil B/m_3\rceil=24,\quad \lceil B/m_4\rceil=40 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:expbound} each $m_i\le B$, so covering is a finite predicate; exhaustive evaluation over $B=0,\dots,117$ and $m\le B$ shows it fails everywhere. Structurally: $\lceil B/m_4\rceil=40$ forces $39m_4<B\le40m_4$ and $\lceil B/m_3\rceil=24$ forces $23m_3<B\le24m_3$; the tightest overlap compatible with the remaining constraints occurs at $m_4=3$, $m_3=5$, forcing $B>117$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Observational indistinguishability]\label{thm:ambig}
Two master profiles, one with master knee $118$ and one with $120$, produce identical values on all four table entries under the exponents $(10,6,5,3)$, while having different master knees.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorems~\ref{thm:120} and~\ref{thm:118}; $118\ne120$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact gauge freedom]\label{thm:gauge}
For $B\in\N$,
\[
\Big(\lceil B/10\rceil=12\ \wedge\ \lceil B/6\rceil=20\ \wedge\ \lceil B/5\rceil=24\ \wedge\ \lceil B/3\rceil=40\Big)\iff B\in\{118,119,120\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:interval} the four conditions are $110<B\le120$, $114<B\le120$, $115<B\le120$, $117<B\le120$; their conjunction is $117<B\le120$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The five-domain table, measured at a single gate and context, determines the master profile only up to a three-element ambiguity, and the exponent vector only up to the corresponding rescaling. The value $120$ is privileged not by the physics but by an unjustified divisibility demand. Any claim that a particular master knee or exponent vector is canonical requires an independent observable.
\end{remark}

\section{Rigidity of exact multiplicativity}\label{sec:rigid}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative rigidity]\label{thm:rigidity}
Let $A,B$ be strictly positive profiles and $m\ge1$. If $\ks(A,\tau)=m\,\ks(B,\tau)$ for every $\tau>0$, then $R_A(mk)=R_B(k)$ for every $k\ge1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $k\ge1$ and put $\tau=R_B(k)>0$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:selfcal}, $\ks(B,\tau)=k$, so the hypothesis gives $\ks(A,\tau)=mk$ and hence $\tau\le R_A(mk)$, i.e. $R_B(k)\le R_A(mk)$. Conversely put $\sigma=R_A(mk)>0$; then $\ks(A,\sigma)=mk$, so $m\,\ks(B,\sigma)=mk$, $\ks(B,\sigma)=k$ and $\sigma\le R_B(k)$. Antisymmetry concludes.
\end{proof}

Thus ``the tax is an exact multiplier at every gate'' is a complete determination: $A$'s retention curve sampled at multiples of $m$ \emph{is} $B$'s retention curve. The internal distribution of mass within each block of $m$ positions is unconstrained; the block totals are rigid.

\begin{corollary}[Composite taxes compose]
$\left\lceil \lceil B/m\rceil/m'\right\rceil=\lceil B/(mm')\rceil$, hence
$\kg(r^{mm'},t)=\left\lceil \lceil \kg(r,t)/m\rceil/m'\right\rceil$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both sides have the same down-set: $\lceil\lceil B/m\rceil/m'\rceil\le n\iff \lceil B/m\rceil\le m'n\iff B\le mm'n\iff \lceil B/(mm')\rceil\le n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Composite delay-after-root law]
For $0\le r<1$, $0<\tau<1$, $d\ge0$, $m\ge1$,
\[
\ks\big(\delayp_d(\geomp(r^m)),\ \tau\big)=d+\left\lceil \frac{\kg(r,1-\tau)}{m}\right\rceil .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:delay}, then Proposition~\ref{prop:trans} and Theorem~\ref{thm:root}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mixed-corpus sandwich]
For $\lambda\in[0,1]$ and nonnegative profiles $A,B$ with reachable gates,
\[
\min\big(\ks(A,\tau),\ks(B,\tau)\big)\le \ks\big(\lambda A+(1-\lambda)B,\ \tau\big)\le \max\big(\ks(A,\tau),\ks(B,\tau)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Retention is affine in $\lambda$, so $R_{\mathrm{mix}}$ lies pointwise between $\min(R_A,R_B)$ and $\max(R_A,R_B)$; Proposition~\ref{prop:dom} transfers this to the knees.
\end{proof}

Mixing corpora therefore cannot produce a knee outside the range of the constituents: the observed spread across domains is not a mixing artefact.

\section{Tail classes}\label{sec:tails}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:heavy}
$R_{\heavyp}(k)=\dfrac{k}{k+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Telescoping: $\frac{1}{(i+1)(i+2)}=\frac1{i+1}-\frac1{i+2}$.
\end{proof}

So the heavy knee at tail budget $t$ is about $1/t$: polynomial in $1/t$, where the geometric knee is logarithmic.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bern}
For $0<r<1$ and any $C\in\N$ there is $n\ge1$ with $Cn\,r^n<1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $s=1/r>1$ and $c=s-1>0$. Bernoulli gives $s^p\ge 1+pc$, so $s^{2p}\ge(1+pc)^2\ge 1+2pc+p^2c^2$. Choosing a natural $p>\max(2C/c^2,1)$ makes $pc^2>2C$, hence $p^2c^2>2Cp$ and $s^{2p}>C\cdot(2p)$. Take $n=2p$ and invert.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tail-class separation]\label{thm:tailsep}
For $0<r<1$ and every $C\in\N$ there is a gate $\tau\in(0,1)$ with
\[
C\,\ks(\geomp(r),\tau)\ \le\ \ks(\heavyp,\tau).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $n$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:bern} and set $\tau=1-r^n$. By Propositions~\ref{prop:trans} and~\ref{prop:cal}, $\ks(\geomp(r),\tau)=n$ exactly. By Lemma~\ref{lem:heavy}, $\tau\le R_{\heavyp}(j)\iff (j+1)r^n\ge 1$; for every $j<Cn$ we have $(j+1)r^n\le Cn\,r^n<1$, so all such $j$ fail the gate, whence $\ks(\heavyp,\tau)\ge Cn$. Reachability follows from $R_{\heavyp}(k)\to1$.
\end{proof}

Across tail classes there is no bounded multiplier, let alone a bounded additive tax. The ``$+4$ versus $\times2$'' question is therefore conditional on all five domains inhabiting the geometric class. This gives an experimental discriminator: a geometric-class domain responds to a doubling of context with an additive shift of the knee, a polynomial-class domain with a multiplicative one.

\section{Measurement grids}\label{sec:grids}

Real measurements test a finite set $G$ of cut-offs; write $\ks_G(w,\tau)=\inf\{k\in G:\ \tau\le \Rw(k)\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Grids never underestimate; brackets]\label{thm:grid}
For nonnegative $w$ with a passing grid point:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\ks(w,\tau)\le \ks_G(w,\tau)$;
\item $\ks_G(w,\tau)=\inf\{k\in G:\ \ks(w,\tau)\le k\}$;
\item if $\ks(w,\tau)\in G$ then $\ks_G(w,\tau)=\ks(w,\tau)$;
\item if $\tau>\Rw(a)$ and $\tau\le \Rw(b)$ then $a<\ks(w,\tau)\le b$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) A passing grid point lies in the full gate set. (2) By Proposition~\ref{prop:persist}, ``$\tau\le \Rw(k)$'' and ``$\ks(w,\tau)\le k$'' define the same set; intersect with $G$. (3) Immediate from (2). (4) The upper bound is minimality of the infimum; the lower bound is Proposition~\ref{prop:spec}.
\end{proof}

Applied to the French data with $\tau=0.98$: $R(36)=0.9795<\tau\le0.9830=R(40)$ gives $36<\ks\le40$. Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:fr3940} this narrows the knee to $\{39,40\}$ under the geometric square-root hypothesis.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{What the theory settles}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Two mechanisms, structurally distinct.} Delay is additive and gate-independent (Theorem~\ref{thm:delay}); root-of-ratio is multiplicative up to a ceiling (Theorem~\ref{thm:root}), exact on multiples (Theorem~\ref{thm:exact}), and cannot be imitated by any fixed additive constant (Corollary~\ref{cor:notroot}).
\item \textbf{The $\times2$ verdict is consistent but not tight.} A square-root decay tax with an English knee of $20$ predicts a French knee in $\{39,40\}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:fr3940}); the reported $40$ requires an unverified parity assumption.
\item \textbf{Exact multiplicativity is rigid.} If the multiplier law holds at every gate, the taxed retention curve is a block dilation of the untaxed one (Theorem~\ref{thm:rigidity}), with no geometric hypothesis.
\item \textbf{The master profile is underdetermined.} The admissible masters are exactly $\{118,119,120\}$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:gauge}); $118$ is the true minimum over all exponent vectors (Theorem~\ref{thm:min118}); $118$ and $120$ are observationally indistinguishable (Theorem~\ref{thm:ambig}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What the theory does not settle}
The theory is conditional on the empirical premise that the domain tails are geometric with the claimed exponent relations, in particular $r_{\mathrm{EN}}=r_{\mathrm{FR}}^2$. Nothing here establishes that premise, and Theorem~\ref{thm:tailsep} shows what is at stake: outside the geometric class the language of fixed taxes has no referent. Nor does the theory explain why the exponents take the values they do; a single gate provides only one equation per domain.

\subsection{Design consequences}
If a domain's tax is additive, the extra cost is a constant and can be amortised; tightening the gate does not worsen it. If it is multiplicative, the extra cost scales with the gate. Gate invariance (Theorem~\ref{thm:gateinv}) means a domain's multiplier can be calibrated once at a convenient gate and transferred. Theorem~\ref{thm:grid} means a coarse grid is safe: it over-provisions, never under-provisions.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{conjecture}[Gate-invariant tax ratio]
For any two domains whose attention profiles have geometric tails with ratios $r_1,r_2$, the measured knee ratio satisfies
\[
\left|\frac{\ks_1(\tau)}{\ks_2(\tau)}-\frac{\log r_2}{\log r_1}\right|\longrightarrow 0\quad\text{as }\tau\to1,
\]
uniformly over corpora.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is Theorem~\ref{thm:gateinv}: the ideal knee factorises into a gate-dependent numerator and a domain-dependent denominator, so the domain multiplier is a pure tail invariant; the only gate dependence left is the sub-unit rounding of Proposition~\ref{prop:acc}. The integer half of the prediction is already proved (Theorem~\ref{thm:twosided}), whence the French knee admits only $39$ or $40$ for an English knee of $20$. What remains open is the empirical premise that both tails are geometric with $r_{\mathrm{EN}}=r_{\mathrm{FR}}^2$. The prediction $|\ks_{\mathrm{FR}}-2\ks_{\mathrm{EN}}|\le 2$ at every context is testable with existing apparatus at zero additional modelling cost.

\begin{conjecture}[Tail-class dichotomy]
Every language/domain profile falls into exactly one of two knee classes --- logarithmic ($\ks=\Theta(\log(1/t))$, geometric tails) or polynomial ($\ks=\Theta(t^{-1/\alpha})$, power-law tails) --- and no natural-language corpus is in the polynomial class at context $1024$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is Theorem~\ref{thm:tailsep}: the two classes are separated by an unbounded factor, not a constant, so a single doubling of the context length distinguishes them --- the geometric class shifts the knee by an additive constant, the polynomial class by a multiplicative one. The ``$+4$ versus $\times2$'' dispute is exactly the question of which class the domains live in.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Further programme.} French at a second context (e.g.\ $512$) on the extended grid, to test gate/context invariance of the ratio; increments at context $4096$, to separate additive from multiplicative context response; additional languages, to see whether the multiplier set is discrete or continuous; an independent observable to break the $\{118,119,120\}$ degeneracy, for instance a sixth domain whose entry is sensitive to $B \bmod 6$; and direct estimation of the tail ratios per domain, converting the conditional theory into an unconditional one.

\section{Conclusion}

The retention knee is a clean, exactly analysable observable supporting exactly two structural tax mechanisms: an additive delay, which shifts it by a gate-independent constant, and a multiplicative decay tax, which scales it by an integer up to a single ceiling step. These are provably different, and no fixed additive constant can imitate a root tax across gates.

Applied to the five-domain table, the theory confirms that a square-root decay tax is consistent with the French knee being double the English one, but sharpens the claim to ``$39$ or $40$'', the residual ambiguity being exactly the parity bit a coarse grid cannot see. It also shows that reconstruction of a single master profile from the table is not unique: the admissible master knees are precisely $118$, $119$ and $120$, so the apparent determination of a canonical master at $120$ is an artefact of an unnecessary divisibility assumption.

Finally, the theory delivers two falsifiable predictions at no modelling cost: the tax ratio between domains must be independent of the gate, and doubling the context must separate logarithmic from polynomial tail classes by the kind of shift it induces.

